      <memberdef kind="function" id="class_a_g_remap_core_1_1_tex_thumbprint_1a6f118e7d941f3a988d69424fe3c364f2" prot="public" static="yes" const="no" explicit="no" inline="no" virt="non-virtual">
        <type>double</type>
        <definition>double AGRemapCore::TexThumbprint::correlation</definition>
        <argsstring>(const std::vector&lt; double &gt; &amp;thumb, const std::vector&lt; std::uint8_t &gt; &amp;stored)</argsstring>
        <name>correlation</name>
        <qualifiedname>AGRemapCore::TexThumbprint::correlation</qualifiedname>
        <param>
          <type>const std::vector&lt; double &gt; &amp;</type>
          <declname>thumb</declname>
        </param>
        <param>
          <type>const std::vector&lt; std::uint8_t &gt; &amp;</type>
          <declname>stored</declname>
        </param>
        <briefdescription>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para><verbatim>embed:rst 
`Pearson correlation`_ between a thumbprint and a stored one :raw-html:`&lt;br /&gt;`
:raw-html:`&lt;br /&gt;`

Mean-centred, so it answers &quot;is this the same PICTURE&quot; rather than &quot;is this the same
brightness&quot;: a re-saved, re-exposed or re-compressed copy still correlates near 1

.. _Pearson correlation: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pearson_correlation_coefficient
</verbatim></para>
<para><parameterlist kind="param"><parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>thumb</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>A thumbprint, as <ref refid="class_a_g_remap_core_1_1_tex_thumbprint_1a61bba32d7ce38368e712de441338d898" kindref="member">of</ref> returns it </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>stored</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>A thumbprint from a <ref refid="class_a_g_remap_core_1_1_tex_thumbprint_1a3e3f12f2576ae3376259c105901c1ed1" kindref="member">Table</ref></para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
<simplesect kind="return"><para>The correlation in [-1, 1], or <computeroutput>0</computeroutput> if the two are different lengths, empty, or either is perfectly flat (a flat image correlates with nothing) </para>
</simplesect>
</para>
        </detaileddescription>
        <inbodydescription>
        </inbodydescription>
